        "id": "char_1",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "SEM",
        "data_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "High-magnification SEM images show the surface of NF covered with very dense nanowires with length of tens of micrometers and average diameter of ~84 nm.",
        "evidence_snippet": "high-magnification SEM images show that the surface of NF is covered with very dense nanowires (Fig. 1c,d), which have a length of tens of micrometers and average diameter of ∼84 nm (Fig. 1e)."
      },
      {
        "id": "char_2",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "TEM",
        "data_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "Dark-field TEM shows nanowire co-axial heterostructure with inner diameter ~66 nm and outer layer thickness ~20 nm.",
        "evidence_snippet": "Fig. 2a clearly shows that the nanowire has a co-axial heterostructure, in which the inner part has a diameter of ∼66 nm and the outer layer has an average thickness of ∼20 nm."
      },
      {
        "id": "char_3",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "HRTEM",
        "data_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "HRTEM shows lattice fringes of 0.62 nm for MoS₂ (002) facet and 0.41 nm for Ni₃S₂ (101) facet.",
        "evidence_snippet": "The outer and inner parts show lattice fringes with distance of 0.62 and 0.41 nm, respectively, which are assigned to the (002) facet of MoS₂ and (101) facet of Ni₃S₂."
      },
      {
        "id": "char_4",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "EDX",
        "data_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "EDX elemental mappings show S uniformly distributed over the whole nanowire while Mo and Ni are dominantly distributed at peripheral and center regions; mass ratio Mo:S is 107:104.",
        "evidence_snippet": "EDX elemental mappings show that S is uniformly distributed over the whole nanowire while Mo and Ni are dominantly distributed at the peripheral and center regions, respectively (Fig. 2e-h)."
      },
      {
        "id": "char_5",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "XRD",
        "data_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "XRD shows strong sharp peaks of hexagonal Ni₃S₂ at 21.754°, 31.104°, 37.779°, 44.330°, 49.733°, 55.161° and weak broadened MoS₂ (002) peak at 14.197° plus Ni peaks.",
        "evidence_snippet": "the strong and sharp XRD peaks at 21.754°, 31.104°, 37.779°, 44.330°, 49.733° and 55.161° are attributed to the (101), (110), (003), (202), (113) and (122) facets of high crystalline hexagonal Ni₃S₂ ... The XRD peak at 14.197° is related to the (002) facet of MoS₂."
      },
      {
        "id": "char_6",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "XPS",
        "data_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "XPS shows Mo 3d peaks at 228.9 eV (5/2) and 232.1 eV (3/2) of MoS₂ with upshift indicating electronic interaction; Ni 2p shows mixed Ni⁰ (852.7, 870.2 eV), Ni²⁺ (853.7, 873.3 eV), Ni³⁺ (856.4 eV); S 2p at 161.7 eV (3/2) and 162.9 eV (1/2).",
        "evidence_snippet": "The peaks at 228.9 and 232.1 eV are assigned to Mo 3d₅/₂ and Mo 3d₃/₂ of MoS₂, respectively ... the 852.7 and 870.2 eV peaks are assigned to Ni 2p₃/₂ and 2p₁/₂ of Ni⁰, while 853.7 and 873.3 eV peaks are attributed to Ni²⁺ ... The peaks at 161.7 and 162.9 eV are assigned to S 2p₃/₂ and 2p₁/₂."
      },
      {
        "id": "char_7",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "contact angle",
        "data_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "Contact angle measurement shows superhydrophilicity with CA of 0° for 1 mol L⁻¹ KOH droplet.",
        "evidence_snippet": "NF, Pt/C-NF, Ni₇S₆ & Ni₃S₂-NF and MoS₂/Ni₃S₂ NW-NF show CAs of 137°, 126°, 98° and 0°, respectively."
      },
      {
        "id": "char_8",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "CV",
        "data_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "CV from 0.465 to 0.565 V vs RHE at scan rates 40-120 mV/s gives double-layer capacitance Cdl of 92.40 mF/cm² used to calculate ECSA of 1885.7 cm².",
        "evidence_snippet": "To evaluate the electrochemical active surface areas (ECSA) of the catalysts, CV was obtained from 0.465 to 0.565 V versus RHE at a series of scan rates of 40, 60, 80, 100 and 120 mV s⁻¹ ... MoS₂/Ni₃S₂ NW-NF catalyst shows a Cdl of 92.40 mF cm⁻² ... The ECSA of MoS₂/Ni₃S₂ NW-NF ... is 1885.7 ... cm²."
      },
      {
        "id": "char_9",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "EIS",
